{"turn": 3, "role": "tool", "result": "# APPRAISAL SUMMARY REPORT\n\n**Summary of Complete Appraisal --- Mixed-Use Retail and Office Property**\n\n**520--534 King Street Charleston, SC 29403**\n\n**Prepared for:** Calverley Heritage Bank 412 Whitaker Street Savannah, GA 31401 Attention: Marcus Prideaux, Senior Vice President -- Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\n**Prepared by:** Pinnacle Valuation Services LLC 185 East Bay Street, Suite 400 Charleston, SC 29401\n\n**File Reference No.:** PVS-2025-0214-CHS\n\n**Effective Date of Value:** February 14, 2025 **Date of Report:** February 14, 2025\n\n**Intended Use:** To assist the client in evaluating collateral for a proposed commercial mortgage loan.\n\n**Intended User:** Calverley Heritage Bank and its successors and/or assigns.\n\nThis is a Summary Appraisal Report prepared in conformity with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice (USPAP). It summarizes the appraiser's analyses, opinions, and conclusions. Supporting documentation is retained in the appraiser's work file. The depth of discussion contained herein is specific to the needs of the client and the intended use stated above. The appraiser is not responsible for unauthorized use of this report.\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n**Property Identification:** 520--534 King Street, Charleston, SC 29403; Tax Map Nos. 460-12-04-007, 460-12-04-008, and 460-12-04-009.\n\n**Property Type:** Mixed-use --- ground-floor retail (three bays, 11,400 SF) and upper-floor office (two stories, 19,600 SF); total gross building area of 31,000 SF.\n\n**Land Area:** 0.38 acres (approximately 16,553 SF).\n\n**Zoning:** GB (General Business), City of Charleston.\n\n**Owner of Record:** Hampstead Village Holdings LLC. The property is currently under contract to Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC.\n\n**Borrower / Prospective Purchaser:** Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC.\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Item                                                   Amount / Figure\n  ------------------------------------------------------ -------------------------------------------\n  Purchase Price (per contract)                          \\$17,800,000\n\n  Total Renovation Budget (per borrower's pro forma)     \\$3,150,000\n\n  Total Project Cost                                     \\$20,950,000\n\n  Current Occupancy                                      68% (21,400 SF of 31,000 SF occupied)\n\n  In-Place Annual Rental Revenue                         \\$415,100\n\n  **Appraised Value --- As-Is**                          **\\$18,200,000**\n\n  **Appraised Value --- As-Stabilized (hypothetical)**   **\\$23,500,000**\n\n  Proposed Loan Amount                                   \\$14,750,000\n\n  Loan-to-Value (As-Is)                                  \\$14,750,000 / \\$18,200,000 = **81.04%**\n\n  Loan-to-Value (As-Stabilized)                          \\$14,750,000 / \\$23,500,000 = **62.77%**\n\n  Loan-to-Cost                                           \\$14,750,000 / \\$20,950,000 = **70.41%**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Extraordinary Assumptions:** (1) The as-stabilized value assumes completion of the planned \\$3,150,000 renovation program within 14 months and lease-up of currently vacant space to 95% occupancy within 24 months from the effective date. (2) No extraordinary assumptions have been made regarding environmental conditions; the property is subject to a recorded land use restriction on Lot 7 (see Section 5).\n\n**Hypothetical Conditions:** The as-stabilized value is based on the hypothetical condition that renovation is complete and the property achieves stabilized occupancy. This is a prospective value estimate as of the anticipated stabilization date, estimated to be Q1 2027.\n\n## 2. Property Description\n\n### 2.1 Site Description\n\n**Location.** The subject property is located in the Upper King Street corridor of Charleston, South Carolina --- a commercially vibrant mixed-use district with strong retail, restaurant, and office demand. The immediate neighborhood is characterized by a mix of adaptive-reuse commercial buildings, boutique retail, creative office tenants, and nationally branded restaurants, all supported by robust pedestrian traffic and tourism.\n\n**Legal Description.** Lots 7, 8, and 9 of the Hampstead Village Plat, recorded in Plat Book 41 at Page 218, in the RMC Office for Charleston County, South Carolina; together with a strip of land formerly known as Anson Lane, vacated by City of Charleston Ordinance No. 2019-084, conveyed to the predecessor in title by quitclaim deed recorded in Deed Book 1128 at Page 443.\n\n**Land Area.** 0.38 acres, approximately 16,553 SF, consisting of three contiguous tax parcels (Tax Map Nos. 460-12-04-007, 460-12-04-008, and 460-12-04-009) plus the vacated Anson Lane right-of-way.\n\n**Shape, Topography, and Frontage.** The assemblage is generally rectangular with level topography and approximately 120 linear feet of frontage on King Street, a major north-south commercial arterial providing direct vehicular and pedestrian access.\n\n**Zoning.** GB (General Business), City of Charleston --- permits retail, office, restaurant, and mixed uses by right. Residential use above the ground floor is permitted. Ground-floor residential use on Lot 7 is restricted by a recorded land use restriction (see Section 5).\n\n**Utilities.** All public utilities are available, including municipal water and sewer, electric service, natural gas, and telecommunications.\n\n**Easements and Encumbrances.** An easement in favor of South Carolina Electric & Gas for underground utility lines, recorded in Deed Book 987 at Page 112, is a standard utility easement that does not materially affect the property's use or value. Additionally, a decorative iron balcony, characteristic of Charleston's historic architectural vernacular, extends approximately 2.5 feet beyond the front building wall over the King Street public right-of-way. The encroachment is reflected as a Schedule B-II exception on the title commitment. The appraiser has treated this as a minor physical consideration that does not materially impair the property's marketability or value, noting that such balcony encroachments are common in Charleston's historic commercial districts and are typically permitted by the City of Charleston under encroachment licenses. However, the existence of the encroachment may require maintenance of a valid encroachment permit, and any city-mandated removal could affect the historic façade. The lender is advised to confirm the status of the encroachment permit in consultation with legal counsel.\n\n**FEMA Flood Zone.** Zone X (area of minimal flood hazard), FEMA Community Panel No. 45019C0418J, effective November 4, 2014. No mandatory flood insurance requirement under federal regulation applies to this property.\n\n### 2.2 Improvements Description\n\n**Year Built / Renovated.** Original structure constructed in 1952; significant renovation completed in 2004.\n\n**Construction.** Masonry and steel frame with brick exterior and stucco detailing; flat roof with a single-ply membrane system.\n\n**Gross Building Area.** 31,000 SF total, configured as follows: Ground Floor --- three retail bays (Bays 1, 2, and 3), each approximately 3,800 SF (11,400 SF total); Upper Floors --- two office stories above grade containing Suite A (9,800 SF) and Suite B (9,800 SF), totaling 19,600 SF. The building is three stories in height.\n\n**Parking.** Limited on-site parking. Tenants and visitors rely on metered street parking and a municipal parking garage located within two blocks.\n\n**Elevator.** One passenger elevator serves all three floors. The elevator has been identified for modernization as part of the renovation budget (\\$475,000).\n\n**HVAC.** Central heating and cooling system is nearing the end of its useful life and has been identified for full replacement in the renovation budget (\\$1,200,000).\n\n**Overall Condition.** Fair to good. Deferred maintenance is noted in the HVAC system, elevator, and portions of the exterior façade. The borrower's renovation budget of \\$3,150,000 addresses these items as follows: HVAC replacement --- \\$1,200,000; elevator modernization --- \\$475,000; façade restoration (including the decorative iron balcony on the King Street elevation) --- \\$625,000; tenant improvement allowances for vacant spaces --- \\$550,000; soft costs and contingency --- \\$300,000. If city-mandated removal of the encroaching balcony were ever required, additional façade work beyond the current budgeted scope could be necessary.\n\n**ADA Compliance.** Generally compliant. The elevator modernization will bring vertical circulation into conformity with current code requirements.\n\n## 3. Tenant and Lease Summary\n\n### 3.1 Rent Roll and Occupancy\n\nThe following table summarizes the property's lease status as of the effective date of value:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Space                Tenant                            SF           Lease Type   Annual Rent     Rent/SF   Lease Expiry   Status\n  -------------------- --------------------------------- ------------ ------------ --------------- --------- -------------- --------------\n  Bay 1 (Ground Fl.)   Lowcountry Provisions Inc.        3,800        NNN          \\$108,300       \\$28.50   12/31/2027     Occupied\n\n  Bay 2 (Ground Fl.)   ---                               3,800        ---          ---             ---       ---            Vacant\n\n  Bay 3 (Ground Fl.)   Harmon & Wren Design Studio LLC   3,800        Mod. Gross   \\$91,200        \\$24.00   06/30/2026     Occupied\n\n  Suite A (2nd Fl.)    Tidemark Consulting Group LLC     9,800        NNN          \\$215,600       \\$22.00   03/31/2029     Occupied\n\n  Suite B (2nd Fl.)    ---                               9,800        ---          ---             ---       ---            Vacant\n\n  **Total**                                              **31,000**                **\\$415,100**                            **68% Occ.**\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nTotal occupied area is 21,400 SF (68%); vacant area is 13,600 SF (32%), comprising Bay 2 (3,800 SF) and Suite B (9,800 SF). The weighted average in-place rent for occupied space is \\$19.40/SF (blended across retail and office). Lowcountry Provisions is a local specialty grocer with stable operations and consistent foot traffic. Harmon & Wren is a boutique interior design studio. Tidemark Consulting Group is the largest tenant by both area and rental income, providing anchor cash flow through its lease term expiring March 31, 2029.\n\n### 3.2 Lease Analysis and Notable Provisions\n\n**Lowcountry Provisions Inc. (Bay 1).** NNN lease at \\$28.50/SF through December 31, 2027. The tenant is responsible for its pro rata share of operating expenses including real estate taxes, insurance, and common area maintenance. The appraiser notes that this lease does not contain an assignment or subletting consent clause; the appraiser does not opine on the legal implications of this omission but notes it is unusual for a lease of this type.\n\n**Harmon & Wren Design Studio LLC (Bay 3).** Modified gross lease at \\$24.00/SF through June 30, 2026. The near-term expiration of this lease --- approximately 16 months from the effective date of value --- is a leasing risk factor. Additionally, the Harmon & Wren lease includes a right of first refusal (\"ROFR\") on the adjacent Bay 2, which is currently vacant. The existence of this ROFR may constrain the borrower's ability to lease Bay 2 to a third-party tenant at optimal market terms, as any bona fide third-party offer must first be presented to Harmon & Wren on matching terms. In the appraiser's as-stabilized projections, this ROFR has been factored into the lease-up timeline for Bay 2, adding an estimated 60--90 days to the projected absorption period to account for the ROFR exercise or waiver process. This ROFR should be considered in any lease-up covenant or stabilization timeline imposed by the lender.\n\n**Tidemark Consulting Group LLC (Suite A).** NNN lease at \\$22.00/SF through March 31, 2029. This is the longest remaining lease term among all tenants and provides stable, predictable cash flow through the anticipated loan maturity period.\n\n**Bay 2 (Vacant).** 3,800 SF ground-floor retail; subject to the Harmon & Wren ROFR. Market rent is estimated at \\$26.00--\\$28.00/SF NNN based on comparable ground-floor retail leases on Upper King Street.\n\n**Suite B (Vacant).** 9,800 SF second-floor office. Market rent is estimated at \\$20.00--\\$23.00/SF NNN based on comparable Class B office leases in the Charleston CBD and Upper King corridor.\n\n## 4. Market Analysis\n\n**Submarket Overview.** Upper King Street is one of Charleston's premier mixed-use corridors, benefiting from strong pedestrian traffic, growing restaurant and retail demand, tourism, population in-migration, and proximity to the College of Charleston and MUSC employment centers.\n\n**Retail Market.** Ground-floor retail vacancy on Upper King Street is approximately 5--8%. Asking rents range from \\$24.00 to \\$32.00/SF NNN depending on frontage quality and condition. The subject's ground-floor retail rents are within the prevailing market range.\n\n**Office Market.** Charleston CBD and Upper King office vacancy is approximately 12--15%. Class B office asking rents are \\$19.00--\\$25.00/SF. The subject's office rents are slightly below the top of the range, which may facilitate lease-up of Suite B.\n\n**Comparable Sales (Summary):**\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Sale   Address                SF       Sale Date   Price          Price/SF   Occupancy\n  ------ ---------------------- -------- ----------- -------------- ---------- -----------\n  1      610 King Street        22,000   Oct. 2024   \\$13,200,000   \\$600      85%\n\n  2      445 Meeting Street     28,500   Jun. 2024   \\$15,675,000   \\$550      92%\n\n  3      312--318 King Street   18,700   Jan. 2025   \\$11,594,000   \\$620      78%\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe subject's implied as-is price of \\$587/SF (\\$18,200,000 ÷ 31,000 SF) is consistent with the comparable range. The as-stabilized price of \\$758/SF (\\$23,500,000 ÷ 31,000 SF) reflects a premium justified by completed renovation and stabilized occupancy.\n\n**Capitalization Rates.** Market cap rates for comparable mixed-use properties range from 5.75% to 6.50%. The appraiser selected a going-in cap rate of 6.25% for the as-is valuation (reflecting lease-up risk and deferred maintenance) and 5.85% for the as-stabilized valuation (reflecting reduced risk upon stabilization).\n\n## 5. Environmental and Physical Considerations\n\n**Phase I ESA Reference.** Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC, Report No. CLR-2025-0119, dated January 28, 2025.\n\n**Environmental Findings.** No Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs) were identified. One Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition (CREC) was identified on Lot 7: a historical petroleum release from a former dry-cleaning operation at the adjacent property (518 King Street) resulted in soil contamination migrating to the southwest corner of Lot 7. The contamination was remediated under South Carolina Department of Health and Environmental Control (SCDHEC) oversight and received a \"No Further Action\" (NFA) letter dated August 12, 2021 (SCDHEC Site ID: SC-29403-0772). A recorded land use restriction (LUR) is associated with the CREC, recorded in Deed Book 1203 at Page 89, Charleston County RMC. The LUR (a) prohibits residential use of the ground floor of Lot 7 (Bay 1) and (b) requires maintenance of a vapor barrier beneath the slab of Bay 1.\n\n**Appraiser's Treatment.** The appraiser has considered the CREC and associated land use restriction in the valuation. Because Bay 1 is currently leased to Lowcountry Provisions Inc. as a specialty grocery --- a commercial use consistent with the LUR --- and because the NFA letter confirms that no further remediation is required, the appraiser has not applied a value reduction for the CREC. However, the LUR runs with the land, and any future conversion of Bay 1's ground floor to residential use would violate the restriction. The appraiser notes that the zoning (GB --- General Business) and highest and best use analysis support continued commercial use, so the restriction does not currently constrain the property's value. The ongoing obligation to maintain the vapor barrier beneath Bay 1 is a compliance requirement that should be addressed in loan documentation.\n\n**Flood Zone.** FEMA Zone X --- minimal flood hazard. No base flood elevation is applicable and no federally mandated flood insurance is required.\n\n**Balcony Encroachment.** As described in Section 2.1, the approximately 2.5-foot decorative iron balcony encroachment over the King Street right-of-way is characterized as a minor physical consideration consistent with Charleston's historic commercial district character. The appraiser does not consider this encroachment to have a measurable adverse impact on the property's market value, given the prevalence of such features in the Charleston market and the general acceptance by the City of Charleston through encroachment permits. However, the appraiser recommends that the lender confirm the existence of a valid encroachment permit or license and evaluate the risk of city-mandated removal in consultation with legal counsel.\n\n## 6. Valuation Analysis\n\n### 6.1 Highest and Best Use\n\n**As Vacant.** Mixed-use commercial development consistent with GB zoning --- retail on the ground floor and office on upper floors. The site's King Street frontage, location, and zoning support this conclusion.\n\n**As Improved.** Continued use as a mixed-use retail and office building. Renovation to address deferred maintenance will maximize value. The CREC/LUR on Lot 7 restricts ground-floor residential conversion but does not impair commercial highest and best use. The current improvements represent a reasonable and appropriate use of the site; renovation is financially feasible and maximizes the property's value.\n\n### 6.2 Income Capitalization Approach --- As-Is Value\n\n**Potential Gross Income (PGI):**\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Component                               Calculation             Amount\n  --------------------------------------- ----------------------- --------------------\n  In-place rent (occupied spaces)         Per rent roll           \\$415,100\n\n  Market rent --- Bay 2                   3,800 SF × \\$27.00/SF   \\$102,600\n\n  Market rent --- Suite B                 9,800 SF × \\$21.50/SF   \\$210,700\n\n  **Total PGI at Stabilized Occupancy**                           **\\$728,400**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Vacancy and Collection Loss (As-Is).** Current physical vacancy stands at 32%. For occupied spaces, a market-based general vacancy and credit loss allowance of 5% has been applied. After adjustment for current vacancy and credit loss, effective gross income reflects the property's present leasing profile.\n\n**Operating Expenses.** For NNN tenants (Lowcountry Provisions and Tidemark Consulting), most operating expenses are passed through to the tenants. For the modified gross tenant (Harmon & Wren), the landlord bears certain expenses. Estimated total landlord operating expenses --- including management fees, structural reserves, and non-recoverable costs --- are approximately \\$85,000 to \\$95,000 per year at current occupancy.\n\n**As-Is Net Operating Income.** Approximately \\$332,000 to \\$345,000, reflecting current 68% occupancy and actual in-place lease terms net of landlord expenses.\n\n**Capitalization Rate.** 6.25% direct capitalization, reflecting lease-up risk, deferred maintenance, and below-stabilized occupancy. The appraiser also performed a discounted cash flow analysis incorporating a projected lease-up and renovation schedule over a 10-year holding period, which supported a value conclusion consistent with the direct capitalization result when reconciled with the property's near-term value-add potential and the \\$17,800,000 contract purchase price.\n\n**Value Indication --- As-Is.** The appraiser has reconciled the income capitalization approach with the sales comparison approach and cost approach (not detailed in this summary) to arrive at a final as-is value opinion of **\\$18,200,000** as of February 14, 2025.\n\n### 6.3 Income Capitalization Approach --- As-Stabilized Value\n\n**Assumptions.** Renovation completed per the \\$3,150,000 budget; stabilized occupancy of 95% achieved; Bay 2 leased at approximately \\$27.00/SF NNN; Suite B leased at approximately \\$21.50/SF NNN. The estimated lease-up period is 18--24 months from the effective date to achieve 95% occupancy.\n\nThe appraiser notes that the right of first refusal held by Harmon & Wren Design Studio LLC on Bay 2 may extend the lease-up period for that space. Any third-party lease offer for Bay 2 must be submitted to Harmon & Wren under matching terms before it can be executed. This ROFR process is estimated to add 60 to 90 days to the Bay 2 absorption timeline. The lender should consider this constraint when establishing lease-up benchmarks or leasing covenants.\n\n**Stabilized Income:**\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Component                           Amount\n  ----------------------------------- ------------------------------\n  Stabilized PGI                      \\$728,400\n\n  Less: Vacancy/Credit Loss (5%)      (\\$36,420)\n\n  Stabilized Effective Gross Income   \\$691,980\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses            (\\$100,000--\\$110,000)\n\n  **Stabilized NOI**                  **\\$582,000--\\$592,000**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Capitalization Rate (As-Stabilized).** 5.85%, reflecting reduced risk upon stabilization, completed renovation, and a stronger occupancy profile.\n\n**Value Indication --- As-Stabilized.** Reconciled to **\\$23,500,000**. This value is predicated on the hypothetical condition that renovation is complete and the property achieves 95% stabilized occupancy. It is a prospective value estimate as of the anticipated stabilization date, estimated to be Q1 2027.\n\n## 7. Reconciliation and Final Value Opinions\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Valuation Scenario               Value Opinion      Effective Date               Basis\n  -------------------------------- ------------------ ---------------------------- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **As-Is Market Value**           **\\$18,200,000**   February 14, 2025            Fee simple, subject to existing leases, current physical condition, and current occupancy (68%)\n\n  **As-Stabilized Market Value**   **\\$23,500,000**   Prospective (est. Q1 2027)   Fee simple, subject to hypothetical condition of completed renovation and 95% stabilized occupancy\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe as-is value reflects the property's current condition, including deferred maintenance items (aging HVAC system, elevator requiring modernization, façade in need of restoration) and below-stabilized occupancy. The property benefits from a strong King Street location, a creditworthy anchor tenant (Tidemark Consulting Group LLC), and favorable market fundamentals in the Upper King Street submarket. The spread between the as-is and as-stabilized values (\\$5,300,000) represents the value-creation opportunity from the planned renovation and lease-up program. The purchase price of \\$17,800,000 is below the as-is appraised value of \\$18,200,000, suggesting a reasonable acquisition basis.\n\n**Key Ratios for Lender Reference:**\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Ratio                  Calculation                   Result\n  ---------------------- ----------------------------- --------------------\n  Proposed Loan Amount                                 \\$14,750,000\n\n  As-Is LTV              \\$14,750,000 / \\$18,200,000   **81.04%**\n\n  As-Stabilized LTV      \\$14,750,000 / \\$23,500,000   **62.77%**\n\n  Loan-to-Cost           \\$14,750,000 / \\$20,950,000   **70.41%**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe above ratios are provided for the lender's convenience and are calculated based on the proposed loan amount as communicated to the appraiser. The appraiser makes no representation regarding the appropriateness of these ratios for underwriting purposes.\n\n## 8. Assumptions and Limiting Conditions\n\n1.  The appraiser assumes responsible ownership and competent management of the property.\n\n2.  The legal description and title information were obtained from public records and the title commitment issued by Prestige Title & Settlement Services LLC (Commitment No. PT-2025-04-1178, effective February 28, 2025). The appraiser assumes title is marketable and insurable.\n\n3.  The appraiser has made no independent survey of the property and relies on the ALTA/NSPS survey dated January 2025 provided by the client.\n\n4.  The appraiser is not an environmental consultant. Environmental information is based on the Phase I Environmental Site Assessment by Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC (Report No. CLR-2025-0119, dated January 28, 2025). The appraiser assumes the environmental information provided is accurate and complete.\n\n5.  The as-stabilized value is subject to the extraordinary assumption that the \\$3,150,000 renovation program will be completed substantially in accordance with the borrower's plans and budget, and that the property will achieve 95% occupancy within the projected timeframe. If this assumption is not realized, the as-stabilized value may differ materially from the opinion stated herein.\n\n6.  The appraiser has inspected the property interior and exterior. Concealed conditions or latent defects not observable during the inspection are not accounted for in this report.\n\n7.  The appraiser assumes compliance with all applicable zoning ordinances, building codes, and land use restrictions, including the SCDHEC land use restriction recorded in Deed Book 1203 at Page 89, Charleston County RMC.\n\n8.  Possession of this report does not confer the right of publication or use for any purpose other than the stated intended use.\n\n9.  The appraiser's compensation is not contingent upon the value conclusion, the occurrence of a subsequent event, or the outcome of the loan transaction.\n\n## 9. Certification\n\nI certify that, to the best of my knowledge and belief:\n\nThe statements of fact contained in this report are true and correct. The reported analyses, opinions, and conclusions are limited only by the reported assumptions and limiting conditions and are my personal, impartial, and unbiased professional analyses, opinions, and conclusions. I have no present or prospective interest in the property that is the subject of this report and no personal interest with respect to the parties involved. I have no bias with respect to the property that is the subject of this report or to the parties involved with this assignment.\n\nMy engagement in this assignment was not contingent upon developing or reporting predetermined results. My compensation for completing this assignment is not contingent upon the development or reporting of a predetermined value or direction in value that favors the cause of the client, the amount of the value opinion, the attainment of a stipulated result, or the occurrence of a subsequent event directly related to the intended use of this appraisal.\n\nMy analyses, opinions, and conclusions were developed, and this report has been prepared, in conformity with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice (USPAP) as promulgated by The Appraisal Foundation.\n\nI have made a personal inspection of the property that is the subject of this report, including both the interior and exterior of the improvements. No one provided significant real property appraisal assistance to the person signing this certification.\n\nI am competent to perform this appraisal assignment by virtue of my education, experience, and professional credentials. I am a South Carolina Certified General Real Estate Appraiser, License No. SC-CGA-04817.\n\nPINNACLE VALUATION SERVICES LLC\n\nBy: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ Name: R. Colton Mallory, MAI, CCIM Title: Senior Managing Director SC Certified General Real Estate Appraiser License No.: SC-CGA-04817 Date: February 14, 2025\n\n## Addenda\n\nThe following addenda are retained in the appraiser's work file as part of the Complete Appraisal Report and are available upon request:\n\n- **Addendum A:** Comparable Sales Detail and Location Map\n- **Addendum B:** Comparable Rental Data\n- **Addendum C:** Subject Property Photographs (exterior, interior, each tenant bay/suite, building systems)\n- **Addendum D:** ALTA/NSPS Survey (excerpt)\n- **Addendum E:** Plat Map --- Hampstead Village Plat (Plat Book 41, Page 218)\n- **Addendum F:** Flood Map Panel (FEMA Zone X designation)\n- **Addendum G:** Appraiser Qualifications\n\nThis Summary Appraisal Report is intended to be read in conjunction with the Complete Appraisal Report. The complete report contains additional detail, supporting data, and analytical workpapers that form the basis of the opinions expressed herein.\n\nCONFIDENTIAL --- PREPARED FOR THE EXCLUSIVE USE OF CALVERLEY HERITAGE BANK AND ITS SUCCESSORS AND/OR ASSIGNS © 2025 Pinnacle Valuation Services LLC. All rights reserved.\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_p7IJK8a11rHvZ3cqdve31489", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/appraisal-summary.docx\"}"}
{"turn": 3, "role": "tool", "result": "**[PHASE I ENVIRONMENTAL SITE ASSESSMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**[EXECUTIVE SUMMARY]{.underline}**\n\n**Prepared in Accordance with ASTM E1527-21**\n\n**Report Number:** CLR-2025-0119\n\n**Date of Report:** January 28, 2025\n\n**Subject Property:** 520--534 King Street Charleston, South Carolina 29403\n\n**Prepared for: Calverley Heritage Bank 412 Whitaker Street Savannah, Georgia 31401**\n\nAttention: Marcus Prideaux, Senior Vice President -- Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\n**Additional User / Intended Reliance Party:** Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC 88 Broad Street, Suite 200 Charleston, South Carolina 29401\n\n**Prepared by:** Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC 445 Rivers Avenue, Suite 300 North Charleston, South Carolina 29405\n\n**NOTICE:** This Executive Summary is extracted from the full Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Report No. CLR-2025-0119 dated January 28, 2025. The full report, including all appendices (regulatory database search results, historical aerial photographs, Sanborn fire insurance maps, site photographs, interview records, and the assessor\\'s qualifications), should be reviewed in its entirety for a complete understanding of the assessment findings and their basis. This Executive Summary alone does not constitute a complete Phase I Environmental Site Assessment under ASTM E1527-21 or 40 CFR Part 312.\n\n**[1. Purpose and Scope]{.underline}**\n\nClearfield Environmental Consulting LLC (\\\"Clearfield\\\") was retained by Calverley Heritage Bank (\\\"CHB\\\" or \\\"User\\\") to conduct a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"Phase I ESA\\\") of the property located at 520--534 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29403 (the \\\"Subject Property\\\"). The purpose of this Phase I ESA is to identify Recognized Environmental Conditions (\\\"RECs\\\"), Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions (\\\"CRECs\\\"), and Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions (\\\"HRECs\\\") in connection with the Subject Property, as those terms are defined in ASTM E1527-21, Standard Practice for Environmental Site Assessments: Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Process.\n\nThis assessment was conducted to support a proposed commercial real estate loan from Calverley Heritage Bank to Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC in connection with the acquisition and renovation of the Subject Property. Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC has been designated as an additional user of and intended reliance party for this report with the prior written consent of Clearfield.\n\nThe Phase I ESA was conducted in accordance with ASTM E1527-21 and is intended to satisfy the \\\"All Appropriate Inquiries\\\" (\\\"AAI\\\") standard under 40 CFR Part 312, thereby qualifying the User for the innocent landowner, contiguous property owner, and bona fide prospective purchaser defenses to liability under the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (\\\"CERCLA\\\"), 42 U.S.C. §§ 9601 *et seq.*\n\nThe scope of services performed in connection with this Phase I ESA included the following:\n\n> • Review of federal, state, tribal, and local environmental regulatory database records, including standard and additional databases as specified by ASTM E1527-21;\n>\n> • Historical use review utilizing aerial photographs (reviewed from 1938 to present), Sanborn fire insurance maps, city directories, and building department records obtained from the City of Charleston Department of Planning, Preservation and Sustainability;\n>\n> • Site reconnaissance, conducted on January 15, 2025, including an interior and exterior inspection of the Subject Property and visual observation of adjoining properties from the Subject Property boundaries;\n>\n> • Interviews with the current property owner, current occupants, and local government officials with knowledge of the Subject Property and surrounding area; and\n>\n> • Evaluation of adjoining and nearby properties for conditions that may affect the Subject Property.\n\n**Limitations:** This assessment did not include the sampling or analysis of soil, groundwater, surface water, air, or building materials. No Phase II Environmental Site Assessment (subsurface investigation) was conducted as part of this engagement. The findings and conclusions set forth herein are based solely on non-invasive methods.\n\n**Data Gaps:** No significant data gaps were identified during the course of this assessment. Historical records were available for the Subject Property from 1938 to the present, and regulatory database searches returned complete results for all standard search distances.\n\n**[2. Subject Property Description]{.underline}**\n\n**Address:** 520--534 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29403\n\n**Legal Description:** Lots 7, 8, and 9 of the Hampstead Village Plat, recorded in Plat Book 41 at Page 218, in the Register of Mesne Conveyances (\\\"RMC\\\") Office for Charleston County, South Carolina; together with that certain strip of land formerly known as Anson Lane, vacated by City of Charleston Ordinance No. 2019-084, conveyed to the predecessor in title by quitclaim deed recorded in Deed Book 1128 at Page 443, RMC Office for Charleston County.\n\n**Tax Map Numbers:** 460-12-04-007 (Lot 7), 460-12-04-008 (Lot 8), and 460-12-04-009 (Lot 9)\n\n**Total Land Area:** Approximately 0.38 acres (approximately 16,553 square feet)\n\n**Improvements:** The Subject Property is improved with a mixed-use commercial building consisting of ground-floor retail space (three bays totaling approximately 11,400 square feet) and upper-floor office space (two stories, approximately 19,600 square feet), for a total gross building area of approximately 31,000 square feet. The building features a masonry and steel-frame construction with a flat membrane roof, which was last replaced in 2017 according to building department records.\n\n**Year Built:** The original commercial structure was constructed in 1952. A significant renovation was completed in 2004, converting the upper floors from storage and warehouse use to Class B office space and upgrading building systems including HVAC, electrical, plumbing, and fire suppression.\n\n**Current Use:** Mixed-use retail and office, as follows:\n\n> • **Ground Floor --- Bay 1** (approximately 3,800 SF; Lot 7): Occupied by Lowcountry Provisions Inc., a specialty grocery retailer.\n>\n> • **Ground Floor --- Bay 2** (approximately 3,800 SF; Lot 8): Currently vacant.\n>\n> • **Ground Floor --- Bay 3** (approximately 3,800 SF; Lot 9): Occupied by Harmon & Wren Design Studio LLC, an interior design firm.\n>\n> • **Second Floor --- Suite A** (approximately 9,800 SF): Occupied by Tidemark Consulting Group LLC, a management consulting firm.\n>\n> • **Second Floor --- Suite B** (approximately 9,800 SF): Currently vacant.\n\n**Physical Description of Lots:** Lot 7 is the southwestern parcel, fronting King Street, containing Bay 1 on the ground floor and a portion of the second-floor area. Lot 8 is the central parcel containing Bay 2 and additional second-floor area. Lot 9 is the northeastern parcel containing Bay 3 and the remainder of the second floor. The former Anson Lane strip runs along the rear (northwest) boundary of Lots 7, 8, and 9 and currently provides rear access and loading for the building.\n\n**Zoning:** GB (General Business), City of Charleston.\n\n**Topography:** The Subject Property is generally flat with minimal grade change across the site. The Subject Property is situated in FEMA Flood Zone X (area of minimal flood hazard) based on FEMA Flood Insurance Rate Map Panel No. 45019C0520J, effective December 4, 2020.\n\n**Utilities:** Public water and sewer service is provided by the Charleston Water System. Electrical service is provided by South Carolina Electric & Gas. An easement for underground utility lines across the rear of the Subject Property is recorded in Deed Book 987 at Page 112, RMC Office for Charleston County.\n\n**[3. Historical Use Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe following historical use summary is based on Clearfield\\'s review of Sanborn fire insurance maps (1902, 1919, 1944, 1955, 1970, and 1989 editions), aerial photographs (reviewed at approximately five-year intervals from 1938 to present), city directories (1948 through 2024), and building department records obtained from the City of Charleston.\n\n**Subject Property Historical Uses:**\n\n**Pre-1952:** The Subject Property was part of the Hampstead Village residential neighborhood. Lots 7, 8, and 9 were each improved with single-family wood-frame residences, consistent with the surrounding residential character of the area as depicted on Sanborn maps and aerial photographs from this period. Anson Lane was a public alley running behind the lots, providing rear access to the residences and adjacent properties.\n\n**1952:** The single-family residences on Lots 7, 8, and 9 were demolished and the original commercial structure was constructed across all three lots. Building department records indicate the structure was initially built for general retail purposes. The 1955 Sanborn map depicts the building as a one-story commercial structure with a partial second-story storage area.\n\n**1952--2003:** The Subject Property was in continuous use for general retail and small commercial office purposes. City directory listings and building department records from this period identify various tenants, including a hardware store (approximately 1956--1971), a clothing retailer (approximately 1972--1989), and miscellaneous small office and retail tenants thereafter. No evidence of hazardous substance use, storage, treatment, or disposal on the Subject Property was identified during this period. No underground storage tanks or above-ground storage tanks were identified in association with any tenant or use during this period.\n\n**2004:** A significant renovation converted the upper floors from storage and warehouse use to Class B office space. Building systems, including HVAC, electrical, plumbing, and fire suppression, were upgraded. Building department permits and inspection records from the 2004 renovation do not reference any environmental incidents, the discovery of contaminated materials, or the removal of underground storage tanks.\n\n**2019:** City of Charleston Ordinance No. 2019-084 vacated the public alley formerly known as Anson Lane. The vacated strip was conveyed to the then-owner of the Subject Property by quitclaim deed recorded in Deed Book 1128 at Page 443, RMC Office for Charleston County.\n\n**2004--Present:** The Subject Property has been in continuous mixed-use retail and office occupancy, consistent with its current configuration.\n\n**Adjacent Property Historical Use --- 518 King Street:**\n\nThe property located at 518 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina (immediately southwest of and adjoining Lot 7 of the Subject Property) is of particular significance to this assessment. Based on city directory listings, building department records, and Sanborn map review, 518 King Street was historically operated as a dry cleaning establishment known as \\\"King Street Cleaners\\\" from approximately 1968 through 2005. Dry cleaning operations at this location involved the use of perchloroethylene (\\\"PCE,\\\" also known as tetrachloroethylene) and other petroleum-based solvents. The dry cleaning facility was closed in 2005, and the building at 518 King Street was subsequently converted to a restaurant, which remains its current use. Environmental contamination associated with the former dry cleaning operation at 518 King Street is discussed in detail in Section 5 below.\n\n**[4. Findings --- Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs)]{.underline}**\n\n**No Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs) were identified in connection with the Subject Property.**\n\nClearfield reviewed applicable federal and state environmental regulatory databases in accordance with ASTM E1527-21 search distance requirements, including: the EPA National Priorities List (\\\"NPL\\\"); the CERCLIS/SEMS database; the RCRA Corrective Action (CORRACTS) and Treatment, Storage, and Disposal Facility (TSDF) lists; the RCRA generators lists (large and small quantity generators); the Emergency Response Notification System (\\\"ERNS\\\"); state-equivalent hazardous waste site and solid waste facility lists; the Leaking Underground Storage Tank (\\\"LUST\\\") and Underground Storage Tank (\\\"UST\\\") databases maintained by the South Carolina Department of Health and Environmental Control (\\\"SCDHEC\\\"); SCDHEC Voluntary Cleanup Program records; SCDHEC Brownfields Program sites; and state spill and release notification databases.\n\nThe Subject Property does not appear on any federal or state environmental database as a site of concern, listed facility, generator, or location of a reported release.\n\nDuring the site reconnaissance conducted on January 15, 2025, no evidence of underground storage tanks, above-ground storage tanks, or hazardous substance storage was observed on the Subject Property or identified in historical records. No evidence of staining, stressed vegetation, illegal dumping, waste disposal, or other indicators of releases or threatened releases of hazardous substances or petroleum products was observed on the Subject Property.\n\n**No Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions (HRECs) were identified in connection with the Subject Property.**\n\n**One (1) Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition (CREC) was identified in connection with the Subject Property**, as described in detail in Section 5 below.\n\n**[5. Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition (CREC) --- Lot 7]{.underline}**\n\n**5.1 Background and Source of Contamination**\n\nA Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"CREC\\\"), as defined by ASTM E1527-21, was identified with respect to Lot 7 of the Subject Property (Tax Map No. 460-12-04-007). A CREC is defined under ASTM E1527-21 as a recognized environmental condition resulting from a past release of hazardous substances or petroleum products that has been addressed to the satisfaction of the applicable regulatory authority, with hazardous substances or petroleum products allowed to remain in place subject to the implementation of required controls.\n\nThe CREC arises from off-site contamination originating at the adjacent property located at 518 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina, which is immediately southwest of and adjoining Lot 7. As described in Section 3 above, 518 King Street was formerly operated as \\\"King Street Cleaners,\\\" a dry cleaning establishment, from approximately 1968 to 2005.\n\nOperations at King Street Cleaners involved the use of perchloroethylene (\\\"PCE,\\\" also known as tetrachloroethylene) and petroleum-based solvents in commercial dry cleaning equipment. A release of PCE and petroleum-based solvents from the former dry cleaning operation resulted in subsurface soil and shallow groundwater contamination that migrated off-site in a generally northeastern direction --- consistent with the local shallow groundwater flow gradient --- toward and into the southwest corner of Lot 7 of the Subject Property.\n\nThe contamination was identified, reported to the South Carolina Department of Health and Environmental Control (\\\"SCDHEC\\\"), and assigned SCDHEC Site Identification Number SC-29403-0772. The SCDHEC file for this site documents the nature and extent of contamination, the remediation activities undertaken, and the regulatory determination described below.\n\n**5.2 Remediation and Regulatory Closure**\n\nRemediation activities were conducted at the source area on the 518 King Street property and at the impacted portion of Lot 7 of the Subject Property under SCDHEC oversight. Remediation included excavation and off-site disposal of impacted soils, in-situ chemical oxidation treatment of residual soil and groundwater contamination, and a multi-year program of groundwater and soil vapor monitoring to assess the effectiveness of remedial activities.\n\nFollowing the completion of remedial activities and a period of post-remediation monitoring demonstrating stable or declining contaminant concentrations, SCDHEC issued a \\\"No Further Action\\\" (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated August 12, 2021, for SCDHEC Site ID SC-29403-0772. The NFA letter concluded that residual contamination levels in soil and groundwater at and beneath the southwest corner of Lot 7 had been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment under a commercial and non-residential use scenario, subject to certain institutional controls.\n\n**Important:** The NFA letter was issued subject to conditions --- specifically, the implementation and maintenance of the institutional controls described in Section 5.3 below. The NFA status is contingent upon continued compliance with these institutional controls. A failure to maintain the required institutional controls could result in SCDHEC reopening the site for additional investigation, remediation, or regulatory action.\n\n**5.3 Institutional Controls --- Recorded Land Use Restriction**\n\nAs a condition of the SCDHEC NFA determination for Site ID SC-29403-0772, a Land Use Restriction (\\\"LUR\\\") was recorded against Lot 7 of the Subject Property in the RMC Office for Charleston County, South Carolina, at Deed Book 1203 at Page 89. The recorded LUR imposes two specific obligations that run with the land and are binding upon all current and future owners, tenants, lessees, successors, and assigns of Lot 7:\n\n> **(a) Prohibition on Residential Use.** The ground floor of Lot 7 --- that is, Bay 1 of the Subject Property, comprising approximately 3,800 square feet and currently occupied by Lowcountry Provisions Inc. as a specialty grocery --- shall not be converted to or used for residential purposes. This restriction is intended to prevent exposure pathways associated with continuous residential indoor occupancy, including potential vapor intrusion of residual volatile organic compounds (\\\"VOCs\\\") from subsurface soil and groundwater into the indoor air of Bay 1 at levels that may exceed residential screening criteria.\n>\n> **(b) Vapor Barrier Maintenance Obligation.** The property owner is required to maintain, in good condition and working order, a vapor barrier system installed beneath the concrete floor slab of Bay 1 (Lot 7, ground floor). The vapor barrier was installed during remediation activities to mitigate the potential for vapor intrusion of residual VOCs --- principally PCE and its degradation products --- from subsurface soil and groundwater into the indoor air of Bay 1. The LUR requires that the vapor barrier not be penetrated, removed, or compromised without prior written approval from SCDHEC. Any renovation, construction, demolition, or alteration activity affecting the floor slab of Bay 1 must include inspection and, if necessary, repair or replacement of the vapor barrier, with prior written notification to SCDHEC.\n\nThe LUR is binding upon all current and future owners, tenants, and successors in interest to Lot 7 and constitutes a covenant running with the land enforceable by SCDHEC.\n\nDuring the site reconnaissance conducted on January 15, 2025, Clearfield observed that the ground floor of Bay 1 is currently occupied by Lowcountry Provisions Inc. as a specialty grocery retailer. This use is consistent with the commercial-use restriction imposed by the LUR. No evidence of vapor barrier damage, slab penetrations, or other conditions suggesting a compromise of the vapor barrier was observed during the site reconnaissance. However, because the vapor barrier is installed beneath the concrete slab, it was not directly visible or inspectable during the reconnaissance. Clearfield recommends that any prospective purchaser confirm the integrity of the vapor barrier through a qualified inspection conducted by a licensed environmental professional prior to or promptly after acquisition, and particularly before any renovation activity affecting the Bay 1 floor slab.\n\n**5.4 Implications for the Subject Property**\n\nThe CREC does not constitute a Recognized Environmental Condition because the contamination has been addressed to the satisfaction of SCDHEC and the site has received an NFA letter. However, the CREC classification under ASTM E1527-21 reflects the fact that residual contamination remains in place at levels that exceed unrestricted use standards and that the continued effectiveness of the institutional controls (the recorded LUR) is necessary for the site to remain protective of human health.\n\nIf the institutional controls are not maintained --- for example, if the vapor barrier is breached or rendered ineffective during renovation or construction activities, or if the ground floor of Bay 1 is converted to residential use in violation of the LUR --- the NFA status could be jeopardized. Such a breach could result in SCDHEC requiring additional subsurface investigation, remediation measures, or other regulatory action at the expense of the then-current property owner.\n\nThe CREC and associated LUR are matters of public record filed with the RMC Office for Charleston County. Clearfield understands from the preliminary title commitment issued for this transaction (Commitment No. PT-2025-04-1178, issued by Prestige Title & Settlement Services LLC) that the LUR recorded in Deed Book 1203 at Page 89 is listed as a Schedule B-II exception to title. Any lender, purchaser, or other party relying on this report should review the recorded LUR in its entirety and consult with legal counsel regarding its implications.\n\n**Note Regarding Planned Renovation:** Clearfield understands from discussions with representatives of Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC that the prospective purchaser intends to undertake a renovation of the Subject Property that may include HVAC system replacement, elevator modernization, and façade restoration. If any renovation activities involve penetration of, alteration to, or work in proximity to the Bay 1 floor slab on Lot 7, care must be taken to avoid compromising the vapor barrier installed beneath the slab. Clearfield recommends that the renovation contractor be made aware of the existence and location of the vapor barrier and of the LUR requirements, and that SCDHEC be notified in writing in advance of any work affecting the Bay 1 slab. Failure to observe these precautions could result in a violation of the LUR and a reopening of the SCDHEC site file, with attendant regulatory and financial consequences for the property owner and, potentially, the lender.\n\n**[6. Phase II Recommendation]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the findings of this Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, Clearfield does **not** recommend a Phase II Environmental Site Assessment (subsurface investigation) for the Subject Property at this time.\n\nThe sole environmental finding identified in this assessment --- the Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition on Lot 7 --- relates to contamination that has been addressed through SCDHEC-supervised remediation and for which an NFA letter has been issued (August 12, 2021, SCDHEC Site ID SC-29403-0772). Residual contamination is managed through recorded institutional controls in the form of the Land Use Restriction recorded in Deed Book 1203 at Page 89. No conditions were observed during the site reconnaissance or identified during the records review that would suggest a change in subsurface conditions or a failure of the institutional controls. Accordingly, no additional subsurface investigation is warranted at this time unless the institutional controls are breached or new information comes to light suggesting a material change in subsurface conditions.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, Clearfield recommends the following actions in connection with the proposed transaction:\n\n> \\(a\\) The prospective purchaser and borrower (Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC) should obtain and review a complete copy of the SCDHEC remediation file for Site ID SC-29403-0772, including all sampling data, remediation reports, groundwater monitoring results, and the NFA letter with all conditions and attachments.\n>\n> \\(b\\) The integrity of the vapor barrier beneath the Bay 1 floor slab should be confirmed through a qualified inspection by a licensed environmental professional prior to or promptly following acquisition of the Subject Property.\n>\n> \\(c\\) Any renovation contractor performing work in or near Bay 1 on Lot 7 should be informed of the existence and location of the vapor barrier and of the requirements of the recorded LUR, and SCDHEC should be notified in writing in advance of any work affecting the Bay 1 floor slab.\n>\n> \\(d\\) The lender (Calverley Heritage Bank) should consider requiring the borrower to maintain ongoing compliance with the LUR as a specific covenant in the loan documents, including provisions addressing the consequences of non-compliance and the lender\\'s remedies in the event of a breach of the institutional controls.\n\n**Note: Calverley Heritage Bank has waived the Phase II Environmental Site Assessment requirement for this transaction based on the CREC status, the issuance of the SCDHEC NFA letter, and the existence of the recorded institutional controls. This waiver is documented in correspondence dated January 22, 2025, between Marcus Prideaux, Senior Vice President -- Commercial Real Estate Lending, Calverley Heritage Bank, and Clearfield.**\n\n**[7. Conclusions and Opinion]{.underline}**\n\nClearfield Environmental Consulting LLC has performed a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment of the Subject Property located at 520--534 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29403, in conformance with the scope and limitations of ASTM E1527-21, *Standard Practice for Environmental Site Assessments: Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Process*.\n\nThe following is a summary of findings:\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Condition**                                            **Finding**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs)               None identified\n\n  Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions (CRECs)   One (1) identified --- petroleum and solvent contamination (principally PCE) from former dry cleaning operation at adjacent 518 King Street, migrating to southwest corner of Lot 7; remediated under SCDHEC oversight; NFA letter issued August 12, 2021 (SCDHEC Site ID: SC-29403-0772); subject to recorded Land Use Restriction (Deed Book 1203, Page 89, RMC Office for Charleston County) requiring maintenance of vapor barrier beneath Bay 1 floor slab and prohibiting residential use of ground floor of Lot 7\n\n  Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions (HRECs)   None identified\n\n  De Minimis Conditions                                    None identified\n\n  Significant Data Gaps                                    None identified\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nIn the professional opinion of Clearfield, the Subject Property is suitable for the proposed acquisition and continued commercial use, provided that the institutional controls associated with the Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition on Lot 7 are maintained in full compliance with SCDHEC requirements and the terms of the recorded Land Use Restriction. Any future change of use, renovation, or alteration affecting the Bay 1 floor slab on Lot 7 should be undertaken with due regard for the vapor barrier and the LUR, as described in Sections 5 and 6 of this Executive Summary and in greater detail in the full Phase I ESA report.\n\n**[8. Qualifications and Limitations]{.underline}**\n\nThis Executive Summary is a condensed extract of the full Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, Report No. CLR-2025-0119, dated January 28, 2025. The full report, including all appendices --- specifically, Appendix A (Regulatory Database Search Results), Appendix B (Historical Aerial Photographs), Appendix C (Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps), Appendix D (Site Photographs), Appendix E (Interview Records), and Appendix F (Environmental Professional Qualifications) --- should be reviewed in its entirety for a complete understanding of the assessment findings and their basis. This Executive Summary does not replace or supersede the full report.\n\nThis assessment was performed for the exclusive use of the identified User (Calverley Heritage Bank) and the additional reliance party (Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC). No other party may rely on this report or this Executive Summary without the prior written consent of Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC. Any unauthorized use or reliance is at the sole risk of the unauthorized party.\n\nThe assessment reflects conditions observed and information available as of the date of the site reconnaissance (January 15, 2025) and the date of the report (January 28, 2025). Environmental conditions may change over time due to natural processes, human activities, changes in regulatory standards, or other factors. Clearfield assumes no obligation to update this report and no responsibility for changes in conditions or information that occur after the date of this report.\n\nThis assessment involved no invasive investigation, sampling, testing, or analysis of soil, groundwater, surface water, air, or building materials. The conclusions are based solely on non-invasive methods, including visual observation, records review, and interviews. The presence or absence of hazardous substances or petroleum products in environmental media cannot be confirmed without sampling and laboratory analysis.\n\nThe findings and conclusions of this assessment are subject to the inherent limitations of the ASTM E1527-21 standard, including limitations associated with the accuracy and completeness of historical records, the reliability of regulatory database information, the scope of visual observations, and the potential for conditions that are not identifiable through non-invasive methods. No Phase I ESA can wholly eliminate uncertainty regarding the potential for recognized environmental conditions in connection with a property.\n\nThis assessment was conducted under the supervision and direction of Dr. Rebecca Stanton, P.G., a Licensed Geologist in the State of South Carolina (Registration No. 1847), who serves as a Senior Environmental Scientist and Principal of Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC. Dr. Stanton meets the qualifications of an \\\"Environmental Professional\\\" as defined in ASTM E1527-21, Section 3.2.26, and 40 CFR § 312.10, by reason of education, training, and experience in environmental site assessment.\n\n**CERTIFICATION AND SIGNATURE**\n\nI, Dr. Rebecca Stanton, P.G., declare that, to the best of my professional knowledge and belief, I meet the definition of Environmental Professional as defined in § 312.10 of 40 CFR Part 312. I have the specific qualifications based on education, training, and experience to assess a property of the nature, history, and setting of the Subject Property. I have developed and performed the all appropriate inquiries in conformance with the standards and practices set forth in 40 CFR Part 312.\n\nCLEARFIELD ENVIRONMENTAL CONSULTING LLC\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Dr. Rebecca Stanton, P.G.\n\nTitle: Senior Environmental Scientist / Principal\n\nLicense: South Carolina Licensed Geologist, Registration No. 1847\n\nDate: January 28, 2025\n\nReport No.: CLR-2025-0119\n\n**CONFIDENTIAL --- This document contains proprietary information prepared by Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC for the exclusive use of Calverley Heritage Bank and Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC. Reproduction or distribution to third parties without the prior written consent of Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC is prohibited.**\n", "tool_call_id": "call_01_frr9g8Nmme8kjCKjTayM8826", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/phase-i-esa-executive-summary.docx\"}"}
{"turn": 4, "role": "assistant", "text": "Now the borrower operating agreement summary and prior counsel markup notes — critical for identifying issues.", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_C9XHlnfipMWxQlB01IUA9250", "name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/borrower-operating-agreement-summary.docx\"}"}, {"id": "call_01_CIZ6sL11PtVpSJX7Bqff5597", "name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/prior-counsel-markup-notes.docx\"}"}], "input_tokens": 37041, "output_tokens": 126, "finish_reason": "completed"}
{"turn": 4, "role": "tool", "result": "**[REDACTED SUMMARY OF AMENDED AND RESTATED OPERATING AGREEMENT OF PALMETTO GATEWAY PARTNERS LLC]{.underline}**\n\n**A South Carolina Limited Liability Company**\n\nEffective as of January 12, 2024, as amended through March 1, 2025\n\nPrepared and provided by counsel for Borrower for the limited purpose of Lender\\'s review in connection with the proposed loan from Calverley Heritage Bank to Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC. This is a redacted summary only and does not constitute the complete Operating Agreement. Certain provisions, including detailed capital account mechanics, tax allocation provisions, indemnification provisions, and exhibits, have been redacted. Lender\\'s counsel should request the full unredacted Operating Agreement for complete review.\n\nTransmitted by: **Cartwright & Lyons LLP**, counsel to Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC\n\nTransmitted to: Calverley Heritage Bank and its counsel, Fielding, Ashworth & Cole LLP\n\n**[Section 1: Formation and Organization]{.underline}**\n\n**1.1 Formation.** Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC (the \\\"**Company**\\\") was formed as a South Carolina limited liability company on January 12, 2024, by the filing of Articles of Organization with the South Carolina Secretary of State. The Company\\'s filing number with the South Carolina Secretary of State is 2024-0011287. The Company\\'s federal employer identification number (EIN) is 84-6213750. The Company was formed under and is governed by the South Carolina Uniform Limited Liability Company Act of 2012, as set forth in S.C. Code Ann. §§ 33-44-101 *et seq.* (the \\\"**Act**\\\"). To the extent the provisions of the Operating Agreement are inconsistent with the Act, the Operating Agreement shall govern to the fullest extent permitted by law.\n\n**1.2 Name and Principal Office**\n\nThe full legal name of the Company is **Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC**. The principal office of the Company is located at 88 Broad Street, Suite 200, Charleston, South Carolina 29401. The Managing Member may change the principal office of the Company from time to time by providing written notice to each Member.\n\n**1.3 Registered Agent**\n\nThe registered agent of the Company for service of process in the State of South Carolina is **Statehouse Registered Agents, Inc.**, located at 1201 Main Street, Suite 410, Columbia, South Carolina 29201. The Managing Member may change the registered agent from time to time in accordance with applicable law.\n\n**1.4 Purpose.** The stated purpose of the Company, as set forth in Section 1.4 of the Operating Agreement, is as follows:\n\n> \\\"to acquire, own, hold, manage, lease, renovate, improve, operate, finance, refinance, and ultimately dispose of the real property commonly known as 520--534 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29403, and all improvements thereon (the \\'Property\\'), and to engage in any and all activities necessary, incidental, or related thereto.\\\"\n\nThe purpose clause is focused primarily on the Property. However, the concluding language---\\\"and to engage in any and all activities necessary, incidental, or related thereto\\\"---is a broad catch-all that is not otherwise limited or defined. The Operating Agreement does not contain any language restricting the Company to single-purpose entity status, nor does it restrict the Company from engaging in activities beyond the ownership and operation of the Property.\n\n**1.5 Term.** The term of the Company commenced on January 12, 2024, and shall continue in perpetuity until dissolved in accordance with the provisions of the Operating Agreement or as otherwise required by applicable law. The Operating Agreement does not establish a fixed dissolution date. There is no provision within the term section conditioning dissolution upon the consent of any lender or other third party.\n\n**[Section 2: Members and Membership Interests]{.underline}**\n\n**2.1 Members.** The Company has three (3) Members as of the date of the most recent amendment (March 1, 2025). The Members, their respective Membership Interests, entity types, jurisdictions of organization, and roles are as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Member**                         **Membership Interest (%)**   **Type**       **Jurisdiction**   **Role**\n  ---------------------------------- ----------------------------- -------------- ------------------ -----------------\n  Graydon Development Group LLC      72%                           LLC            South Carolina     Managing Member\n\n  Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP   18%                           LP             Delaware           Passive Member\n\n  Harborline Capital Advisors LLC    10%                           LLC            Delaware           Passive Member\n\n  **Total**                          **100%**                                                        \n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe addresses of the Members for purposes of notice under the Operating Agreement are as follows:\n\n> **Graydon Development Group LLC** 88 Broad Street, Suite 200 Charleston, South Carolina 29401\n>\n> **Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP** 301 Fayette Street, Suite 700 Norfolk, Virginia 23510\n>\n> **Harborline Capital Advisors LLC** 1900 Market Street, Suite 1220 Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 19103\n\nThe following additional information regarding the Members is set forth in the Operating Agreement or has been disclosed by the Members in connection with this summary:\n\n**Graydon Development Group LLC (\\\"GDG\\\")** was formed on March 8, 2017, as a South Carolina limited liability company. GDG\\'s sole member and sole manager is **Neil Ashford Graydon**, an individual residing at 14 Church Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29401. Mr. Graydon exercises sole and exclusive control over GDG.\n\n**Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP (\\\"Tidewater\\\")** is a Delaware limited partnership. The general partner of Tidewater is **Tidewater GP Holdings LLC**, a Delaware limited liability company. Tidewater GP Holdings LLC has the exclusive authority to manage and direct the affairs of Tidewater, including its investments and its Membership Interest in the Company.\n\n**Harborline Capital Advisors LLC (\\\"Harborline\\\")** is a Delaware limited liability company. The members and managers of Harborline have not been disclosed in this summary. \\[Redacted: members/managers of Harborline not disclosed.\\]\n\n**2.2 Capital Contributions**\n\nThe Members made initial capital contributions upon the formation of the Company. Pursuant to the Operating Agreement, the Members have committed to fund total equity of **\\$6,200,000** for the acquisition and renovation of the Property at 520--534 King Street, Charleston, South Carolina 29403. Capital contributions are to be made in proportion to each Member\\'s respective Membership Interest, unless otherwise agreed in writing by all Members. The committed capital contributions are as follows:\n\n> • **GDG**: 72% of \\$6,200,000 = **\\$4,464,000**\n>\n> • **Tidewater**: 18% of \\$6,200,000 = **\\$1,116,000**\n>\n> • **Harborline**: 10% of \\$6,200,000 = **\\$620,000**\n\nThe Operating Agreement provides that additional capital contributions may be called by the Managing Member upon not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to all Members. Members who fail to fund additional capital calls within the required period are subject to dilution of their Membership Interest in accordance with a dilution formula set forth in the Operating Agreement. \\[Redacted: Detailed dilution mechanics omitted from this summary.\\]\n\nThe Operating Agreement does not appear to require unanimous consent or approval of a supermajority of Members for the issuance of capital calls. Rather, the Managing Member has unilateral authority to determine the timing, amount, and purpose of additional capital calls, subject only to the overall purpose clause of the Company and any limitations imposed by the annual budget approval process described in Section 3.2.\n\n**2.3 Capital Accounts**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Capital account maintenance provisions omitted from this summary. Borrower\\'s counsel notes that capital accounts are maintained in accordance with Treasury Regulations Section 1.704-1(b)(2)(iv).\\]\n\n**[Section 3: Management]{.underline}**\n\n**3.1 Managing Member**\n\nGraydon Development Group LLC (\\\"**GDG**\\\") serves as the Managing Member of the Company. The Operating Agreement provides that GDG has \\\"exclusive and sole authority to manage the day-to-day business and affairs of the Company,\\\" subject only to those matters requiring the approval of the Members as set forth in Section 3.2 (Major Decisions). Without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the Operating Agreement vests the Managing Member with the authority to perform the following functions on behalf of the Company:\n\n> \\(a\\) Negotiating and executing leases of space within the Property on behalf of the Company;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Engaging and overseeing contractors, consultants, architects, engineers, and property managers in connection with the renovation, improvement, and operation of the Property;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Making capital calls on the Members in accordance with Section 2.2;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Opening and maintaining bank accounts in the name of the Company at such financial institutions as the Managing Member shall determine;\n>\n> \\(e\\) Executing loan documents, promissory notes, deeds of trust, mortgages, security agreements, guaranties, and related instruments on behalf of the Company;\n>\n> \\(f\\) Making distributions to the Members in accordance with Section 5 of the Operating Agreement;\n>\n> \\(g\\) Preparing and filing (or causing to be prepared and filed) federal, state, and local tax returns and making tax elections on behalf of the Company;\n>\n> \\(h\\) Engaging legal counsel, accountants, appraisers, and other professionals on behalf of the Company as the Managing Member deems necessary or appropriate.\n\nNeil Ashford Graydon, as the sole member and sole manager of GDG, has the authority to act on behalf of GDG in its capacity as Managing Member of the Company. The Operating Agreement does not impose any requirement for GDG to appoint or maintain an independent director, independent manager, or springing member. There is no provision within the Operating Agreement requiring the consent of any independent party prior to the Company filing a voluntary petition under the United States Bankruptcy Code or any state insolvency proceeding. The Operating Agreement does not contain any bankruptcy remoteness provisions or any requirement that the Company maintain its existence as a single-purpose entity separate and apart from its Members.\n\n**3.2 Major Decisions Requiring Member Approval**\n\nNotwithstanding the Managing Member\\'s broad authority as described in Section 3.1, the Operating Agreement provides that the following \\\"**Major Decisions**\\\" require the prior written approval of Members holding at least **seventy-five percent (75%)** of the outstanding Membership Interests:\n\n> \\(i\\) The sale, exchange, or other disposition of all or substantially all of the Property;\n>\n> \\(ii\\) Any financing or refinancing of the Property in an amount in excess of \\$1,000,000;\n>\n> \\(iii\\) Approval of the annual operating budget, if the proposed budget reflects a variance exceeding 10% of the prior year\\'s approved budget;\n>\n> \\(iv\\) Any single capital expenditure in excess of \\$250,000 that is not included in an approved annual budget;\n>\n> \\(v\\) The admission of new Members to the Company;\n>\n> \\(vi\\) Any amendment to the Operating Agreement;\n>\n> \\(vii\\) The dissolution or winding up of the Company;\n>\n> \\(viii\\) The commencement of any litigation or arbitration in which the amount in controversy exceeds \\$100,000; and\n>\n> \\(ix\\) The entry into any agreement or transaction with an Affiliate of the Managing Member, except to the extent such agreement or transaction is on arms\\'-length terms and has been disclosed to all Members.\n\nBecause GDG holds 72% of the outstanding Membership Interests, and the threshold for Major Decisions is 75%, GDG alone cannot approve any Major Decision. At least one Passive Member must join GDG in approving any Major Decision. Conversely, no combination of the two Passive Members (holding 18% and 10%, respectively, for a combined 28%) can reach the 75% threshold without GDG\\'s participation, effectively granting GDG a veto over all Major Decisions.\n\nThe list of Major Decisions does not include the filing of a voluntary petition for bankruptcy, insolvency, or reorganization. This decision is not subject to any heightened approval requirement under the Operating Agreement.\n\n**3.3 Officers.** The Operating Agreement provides that the Managing Member may designate one or more officers of the Company. As of the date of this summary, no officers have been formally appointed. Neil Ashford Graydon acts in the capacity of the Company\\'s authorized representative through his role as sole member and manager of GDG, the Managing Member. All documents executed on behalf of the Company are executed by Mr. Graydon in his capacity as sole member and manager of GDG.\n\n**3.4 Compensation of Managing Member**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Managing Member compensation and fee provisions omitted from this summary. Summary note: GDG is entitled to an asset management fee and a development management fee, the terms of which are detailed in the unredacted agreement. Borrower\\'s counsel will make the relevant fee provisions available upon request.\\]\n\n**[Section 4: Transfer Restrictions]{.underline}**\n\n**4.1 General Restriction on Transfers**\n\nNo Member may sell, assign, transfer, pledge, hypothecate, encumber, or otherwise dispose of (collectively, \\\"**Transfer**\\\") all or any portion of its Membership Interest in the Company without the prior written consent of the Managing Member and Members holding at least seventy-five percent (75%) of the Membership Interests, calculated by excluding the Transferring Member\\'s own interest from the denominator used in determining the 75% threshold. Any Transfer made in violation of this Section 4 shall be void and of no force or effect, and the Company and the remaining Members shall not be obligated to recognize or give effect to any such attempted Transfer. The transfer restrictions in this Section 4 apply exclusively to Transfers of Membership Interests in the Company and do not purport to restrict or address changes in the internal or upstream ownership, management, or control of any Member entity.\n\n**4.2 Permitted Transfers**\n\nNotwithstanding the general restriction set forth in Section 4.1, the following Transfers are designated as \\\"**Permitted Transfers**\\\" and do not require the consent of the Managing Member or any other Member:\n\n> \\(a\\) Transfers by any Member to an Affiliate of such Member, as defined in Section 4.5, provided that the transferee agrees in writing to be bound by the terms of the Operating Agreement;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Transfers by GDG to any trust established by Neil Ashford Graydon for estate planning purposes, provided that Neil Ashford Graydon retains voting and management control over the transferred Membership Interest during his lifetime;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Transfers by Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP directed by its general partner, Tidewater GP Holdings LLC, including transfers to any successor fund managed by Tidewater GP Holdings LLC or any Affiliate thereof, without the consent of any other Member;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Transfers among existing Members.\n\nSection 4.2(c) is particularly broad in scope. It grants Tidewater GP Holdings LLC the sole authority to direct Transfers of Tidewater\\'s 18% Membership Interest, not only to Affiliates of Tidewater but also to any \\\"successor fund\\\" managed by Tidewater GP Holdings LLC or any Affiliate of Tidewater GP Holdings LLC. The term \\\"successor fund\\\" is not defined in the Operating Agreement. This provision could result in the Transfer of Tidewater\\'s 18% Membership Interest to a fund entity that is under substantially different economic beneficial ownership than Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP, so long as Tidewater GP Holdings LLC (or an Affiliate thereof) serves as the manager or general partner of such successor fund. No consent from GDG, Harborline, or any third party is required for such a Transfer.\n\nThe Operating Agreement does not contain any provision restricting or requiring consent for changes in the internal ownership, control, or management of any Member at the entity level. By way of illustration:\n\n> • Neil Ashford Graydon could sell or transfer his membership interest in GDG, the 72% Managing Member, to a third party. Because the Membership Interest in the Company would continue to be held in the name of GDG, no Transfer of a Membership Interest in the Company would occur, and the restrictions in Section 4.1 would not be triggered.\n>\n> • Tidewater GP Holdings LLC, the general partner of Tidewater, could itself be acquired by, merged with, or replaced by a new entity. Such a change would effectively alter who controls the 18% Membership Interest held by Tidewater, but would not constitute a Transfer of a Membership Interest in the Company.\n>\n> • The internal membership or management of Harborline Capital Advisors LLC could change entirely without triggering the Transfer restrictions, because the Membership Interest in the Company would remain registered in the name of Harborline.\n\nThe Operating Agreement contains no \\\"change of control\\\" provision that would capture indirect or upstream transfers or that would require notice to, or consent from, the Company or its Members in connection with any such change.\n\n**4.3 Right of First Offer**\n\nIf any Member desires to Transfer all or any portion of its Membership Interest other than pursuant to a Permitted Transfer, such Member (the \\\"**Offering Member**\\\") must first deliver to the Company and the remaining Members a written offer (the \\\"**Transfer Notice**\\\") setting forth the proposed price, terms, and conditions of the proposed Transfer and the identity of the proposed third-party transferee. The remaining Members shall have the right, exercisable within thirty (30) days following receipt of the Transfer Notice, to elect to purchase the offered Membership Interest pro rata in proportion to their respective Membership Interests (excluding the Offering Member\\'s interest) at the same price and on the same terms set forth in the Transfer Notice. If the remaining Members decline or fail to exercise their right of first offer within such thirty (30) day period, the Offering Member may consummate the proposed Transfer to the proposed third-party transferee within ninety (90) days of the expiration of the right of first offer period, provided that such Transfer is on terms no more favorable to the transferee than those set forth in the Transfer Notice.\n\n**4.4 Tag-Along and Drag-Along Rights**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Tag-along and drag-along provisions summarized as follows: If GDG proposes to Transfer 50% or more of its Membership Interest to a third party, the Passive Members have tag-along rights permitting them to participate in such Transfer on the same price and terms. If Members holding 80% or more of the outstanding Membership Interests approve a sale of the Company or the Property to a third party, they may require all other Members to participate in such sale on the same terms and conditions (drag-along). Detailed mechanics redacted.\\]\n\n**4.5 Definition of \\\"Affiliate\\\"**\n\nThe Operating Agreement defines \\\"**Affiliate**\\\" as follows:\n\n> \\\"with respect to any Person, any other Person that directly or indirectly Controls, is Controlled by, or is under common Control with, such Person. \\'Control\\' means the possession, directly or indirectly, of the power to direct or cause the direction of the management and policies of a Person, whether through ownership of voting securities, by contract, or otherwise.\\\"\n\nThis is a standard \\\"control\\\"-based Affiliate definition. However, the Permitted Transfer provision in Section 4.2(c) extends beyond transfers to Affiliates of Tidewater --- it permits transfers to \\\"any successor fund managed by Tidewater GP Holdings LLC or any Affiliate thereof.\\\" A successor fund managed by an Affiliate of Tidewater GP Holdings LLC could be under substantially different ultimate economic ownership than Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP itself, yet such a transfer would qualify as a Permitted Transfer not requiring any consent. The interaction of the Affiliate definition with the Permitted Transfer carve-outs creates a broad pathway for the transfer of Tidewater\\'s 18% interest outside the ordinary consent requirements.\n\n**[Section 5: Distributions]{.underline}**\n\n**5.1 Distribution Waterfall**\n\nDistributable Cash Flow, defined as net cash flow from operations after payment of operating expenses, debt service, and required reserves (but before capital expenditures funded from reserves), shall be distributed to the Members in the following order of priority:\n\n> **First**, to all Members, pro rata in accordance with their respective Membership Interests, until each Member has received cumulative distributions under this tier equal to an eight percent (8%) per annum preferred return on such Member\\'s unreturned capital contributions (the \\\"**Preferred Return**\\\"). The Preferred Return shall accrue and compound annually to the extent not distributed in any given period.\n>\n> **Second**, to all Members, pro rata in accordance with their respective Membership Interests, until each Member has received cumulative distributions under this tier and all prior tiers equal to one hundred percent (100%) of such Member\\'s aggregate capital contributions.\n>\n> **Third**, thereafter, eighty percent (80%) to GDG and twenty percent (20%) to the Passive Members. The Passive Members\\' share of the Third tier shall be allocated among the Passive Members pro rata based on their respective Passive Member Membership Interests (i.e., Tidewater receives 20% × 18/28 ≈ 12.86% and Harborline receives 20% × 10/28 ≈ 7.14%) (the \\\"**Promote**\\\").\n\nThe Third tier reflect a promote or carried interest structure for GDG, the Managing Member, which is typical in real estate joint venture arrangements.\n\n**5.2 Distributions from Capital Events**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Capital event distribution provisions (including distributions from sale, refinancing, condemnation, or casualty proceeds) summarized as follows: Capital event proceeds are distributed in the same waterfall priority as Section 5.1, except that the Third tier promote split is seventy/thirty (70% to GDG and 30% to Passive Members) for capital events. Detailed capital event distribution mechanics, including clawback provisions, are redacted.\\]\n\n**5.3 Tax Distributions**\n\n\\[Redacted --- The Operating Agreement provides that the Company shall make quarterly tax distributions to the Members in amounts estimated to be sufficient to cover each Member\\'s federal and state tax liabilities arising from allocations of Company taxable income for such quarter. Tax distributions are treated as advances against distributions otherwise payable under Section 5.1 and shall reduce amounts otherwise distributable to each recipient Member. Detailed provisions redacted.\\]\n\n**[Section 6: Accounting and Reports]{.underline}**\n\n**6.1 Books and Records**\n\nThe Managing Member shall maintain, or cause to be maintained, complete and accurate books and records of the Company at the Company\\'s principal office located at 88 Broad Street, Suite 200, Charleston, South Carolina 29401. Each Member and its duly authorized representatives shall have the right to inspect and copy such books and records during normal business hours upon reasonable prior written notice to the Managing Member. The books and records of the Company shall be maintained on the accrual basis of accounting in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles consistently applied.\n\n**6.2 Financial Reporting**\n\nThe Managing Member shall cause to be prepared and delivered to each Member the following financial reports:\n\n> \\(a\\) **Quarterly Financial Statements**: Unaudited quarterly financial statements of the Company, including a balance sheet and statement of income and cash flows, within forty-five (45) days after the end of each fiscal quarter;\n>\n> \\(b\\) **Annual Financial Statements**: Annual financial statements of the Company, reviewed (but not audited) by an independent certified public accountant selected by the Managing Member, within ninety (90) days after the end of each fiscal year; and\n>\n> \\(c\\) **Schedule K-1s**: IRS Schedule K-1 (Form 1065) for each Member, within seventy-five (75) days after the end of each fiscal year.\n\nThe Operating Agreement requires only \\\"reviewed\\\" financial statements on an annual basis --- not audited financial statements. This standard of financial reporting is lower than the audited financial statement requirement that may be imposed by the proposed loan documents.\n\n**6.3 Fiscal Year**\n\nThe fiscal year of the Company is the calendar year, commencing January 1 and ending December 31 of each year.\n\n**[Section 7: Exculpation and Indemnification]{.underline}**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Exculpation and indemnification provisions omitted from this summary. Summary note: The Operating Agreement contains standard provisions exculpating the Managing Member and its Affiliates from liability to the Company or any Member for any act or omission taken in good faith, except for liability arising from fraud, willful misconduct, or gross negligence. The Operating Agreement further provides for indemnification of the Managing Member and its Affiliates by the Company from Company assets for claims arising out of or relating to the Managing Member\\'s service in such capacity, subject to the same carve-outs for fraud, willful misconduct, and gross negligence.\\]\n\n**[Section 8: Dissolution and Winding Up]{.underline}**\n\n**8.1 Events of Dissolution**\n\nThe Company shall be dissolved upon the earliest to occur of the following events:\n\n> \\(a\\) The sale or other disposition of all or substantially all of the Property and the distribution of all net proceeds therefrom to the Members in accordance with Section 5;\n>\n> \\(b\\) The prior written consent of Members holding at least seventy-five percent (75%) of the outstanding Membership Interests;\n>\n> \\(c\\) The entry of a decree of judicial dissolution under S.C. Code Ann. § 33-44-701; or\n>\n> \\(d\\) The occurrence of any event that makes it unlawful for the Company to continue its business.\n\nThe Operating Agreement does not include any provision requiring the consent of any lender, independent director, independent manager, or other third party as a condition to the voluntary dissolution of the Company. There is no separateness covenant within the dissolution provisions or elsewhere in the Operating Agreement providing that the Company shall not dissolve, liquidate, or wind up its affairs without the prior written consent of the Company\\'s secured lender. As noted above, the 75% approval threshold for dissolution under Section 8.1(b) requires GDG\\'s participation but does not require unanimous consent of all Members or consent of an independent party.\n\n**8.2 Winding Up**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Winding up provisions omitted from this summary. Summary note: Upon dissolution, the Managing Member (or a liquidating trustee appointed by Members holding a majority of the Membership Interests, if GDG is unable or unwilling to serve) shall wind up the Company\\'s affairs, liquidate its assets in an orderly manner, discharge or make provision for the Company\\'s liabilities to creditors, and distribute remaining assets to the Members in accordance with their positive capital account balances.\\]\n\n**[Section 9: Miscellaneous Provisions]{.underline}**\n\n**9.1 Amendments.** The Operating Agreement may be amended only by a written instrument signed by the Managing Member and Members holding at least seventy-five percent (75%) of the outstanding Membership Interests; *provided, however*, that no amendment that would (i) alter the economic rights of any Member, including but not limited to such Member\\'s rights under the distribution waterfall set forth in Section 5, or (ii) increase any Member\\'s capital contribution obligation beyond amounts previously committed, shall be effective without the prior written consent of each Member so affected. The Operating Agreement does not contain any provision requiring the consent of any lender or other third-party creditor as a condition to amendments.\n\n**9.2 Governing Law**\n\nThe Operating Agreement is governed by and shall be construed in accordance with the laws of the State of South Carolina, without regard to the conflicts of laws principles thereof.\n\n**9.3 Notices.** All notices, demands, consents, approvals, and other communications required or permitted under the Operating Agreement shall be in writing and shall be deemed duly given when delivered by hand, sent by nationally recognized overnight courier service, or mailed by certified mail, return receipt requested, postage prepaid, addressed to the relevant Member at the address set forth in Section 2.1 or at such other address as such Member may designate by written notice to the Company and the other Members. Email notice is permitted for routine business communications but shall not be effective for notices of default, capital calls, or any Major Decision requiring Member approval.\n\n**9.4 Entire Agreement**\n\nThe Operating Agreement, together with all exhibits and schedules thereto, constitutes the entire agreement among the Members with respect to the subject matter hereof and supersedes all prior agreements, understandings, negotiations, and discussions, whether written or oral, among the Members relating to the Company.\n\n**9.5 Severability.** If any provision of the Operating Agreement is held to be invalid, illegal, or unenforceable in any respect, such invalidity, illegality, or unenforceability shall not affect any other provision of the Operating Agreement, and the Operating Agreement shall be construed as if such invalid, illegal, or unenforceable provision had never been contained therein.\n\n**9.6 Dispute Resolution**\n\n\\[Redacted --- Dispute resolution provisions omitted from this summary. Summary note: The Operating Agreement contains a mandatory mediation provision, requiring the Members to submit any dispute arising under the Operating Agreement to mediation in Charleston, South Carolina, prior to initiating any other form of dispute resolution. If mediation is unsuccessful, the dispute shall be resolved by binding arbitration in Charleston, South Carolina, administered under the AAA Commercial Arbitration Rules then in effect. Detailed procedural provisions redacted.\\]\n\n**[Section 10: Signature Page Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe Amended and Restated Operating Agreement was originally executed as of January 12, 2024, by the following parties:\n\n**GRAYDON DEVELOPMENT GROUP LLC**, a South Carolina limited liability company\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Neil Ashford Graydon\n\nTitle: Sole Member and Manager\n\n**TIDEWATER OPPORTUNITY FUND II LP**, a Delaware limited partnership\n\nBy: Tidewater GP Holdings LLC, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: \\[Redacted\\]\n\nTitle: Authorized Signatory\n\n**HARBORLINE CAPITAL ADVISORS LLC**, a Delaware limited liability company\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: \\[Redacted\\]\n\nTitle: Managing Member\n\nThe amendment dated March 1, 2025, was executed by all three Members listed above. The specific terms and modifications effected by such amendment have not been summarized in this document. Borrower\\'s counsel will make the amendment available for review upon request.\n\n**[Appendix: Redaction Notice and Disclaimer]{.underline}**\n\nThis Redacted Summary has been prepared by Cartwright & Lyons LLP, counsel to Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC (the \\\"Borrower\\\"), for the limited purpose of facilitating the review of the Borrower\\'s organizational structure by Calverley Heritage Bank (the \\\"Lender\\\") and its counsel, Fielding, Ashworth & Cole LLP, in connection with the proposed \\$14,750,000 commercial mortgage loan from the Lender to the Borrower.\n\nThis summary is not a substitute for review of the complete Amended and Restated Operating Agreement. Redacted provisions include, without limitation: (i) detailed capital account maintenance and adjustment provisions; (ii) tax allocation provisions, including provisions relating to allocations under Sections 704(b) and 704(c) of the Internal Revenue Code; (iii) indemnification provisions, including procedures for claims and advancement of expenses; (iv) detailed distribution mechanics for capital events, including clawback and true-up provisions; (v) dispute resolution procedures, including mediation and arbitration mechanics; (vi) tag-along and drag-along mechanics, including timing, notice, and pricing provisions; and (vii) all exhibits and schedules to the Operating Agreement, including Member contribution schedules, forms of assignment and assumption, and forms of joinder agreement.\n\nCalverley Heritage Bank and its counsel are advised to request the full unredacted Operating Agreement, including all exhibits and schedules thereto, for purposes of confirming compliance with any single-purpose entity requirements, separateness covenants, bankruptcy remoteness provisions, or other organizational requirements imposed by the loan documents. Compliance with such requirements cannot be confirmed on the basis of this Redacted Summary alone.\n\nThis Redacted Summary is provided solely for informational purposes and shall not be deemed to create any attorney-client relationship between Cartwright & Lyons LLP and the Lender or its counsel. Cartwright & Lyons LLP represents the Borrower exclusively in this matter.\n\n*\\[END OF REDACTED SUMMARY\\]*\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_C9XHlnfipMWxQlB01IUA9250", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/borrower-operating-agreement-summary.docx\"}"}
{"turn": 4, "role": "tool", "result": "**FIELDING, ASHWORTH & COLE LLP** Attorneys at Law Charleston \\| Savannah\n\nPRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL --- ATTORNEY WORK PRODUCT\n\n**INTERNAL MEMORANDUM**\n\n**TO:** Jordan Vickers, Associate, Charleston Office\n\n**FROM:** Catherine \\\"Kit\\\" Ashworth, Partner, Real Estate Practice Group Chair\n\n**DATE:** March 18, 2025\n\n**RE: Markup Notes --- Template SC Commercial Mortgage and Security Agreement for Calverley Heritage Bank / Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC Loan (\\$14,750,000) --- 520--534 King Street, Charleston, SC 29403**\n\nJordan --- I\\'ve reviewed our firm\\'s standard South Carolina commercial mortgage template against the executed term sheet, title commitment (PT-2025-04-1178), borrower\\'s operating agreement summary, rent roll and lease abstracts, and the Phase I ESA executive summary from Clearfield Environmental Consulting LLC. Below are my section-by-section notes and redline comments for your use in preparing the initial draft. Please address each item and flag anything you cannot resolve for discussion at our Wednesday call with Marcus Prideaux at Calverley Heritage Bank. Target delivery of first draft to the client is April 1, 2025, for a projected closing of April 15, 2025.\n\n**[1. Recitals and Defined Terms]{.underline}**\n\nCustomize the template recitals as follows. The borrower\\'s full legal name is Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC, a South Carolina limited liability company (SC SOS Filing No. 2024-0011287), with its principal office at 88 Broad Street, Suite 200, Charleston, SC 29401. The lender is Calverley Heritage Bank, a Georgia state-chartered commercial bank, with its principal office at 412 Whitaker Street, Savannah, GA 31401.\n\nThe loan amount is **\\$14,750,000**. Note the bifurcated structure --- the initial advance is \\$12,500,000 at closing and the renovation holdback is \\$2,250,000. Define both **\\\"Initial Advance\\\"** and **\\\"Renovation Holdback\\\"** separately in the definitions section, and describe the conditions for disbursement of the holdback by cross-reference to the renovation disbursement provisions in the mortgage body.\n\nThe recitals should reference each Loan Document by name: the Promissory Note, the Guaranty Agreement (from Neil Ashford Graydon), the Environmental Indemnity Agreement, and the Assignment of Leases and Rents. On that last point --- I prefer incorporating the assignment of rents into the mortgage body rather than executing it as a separate instrument for this deal. That approach reduces recording costs and streamlines enforcement. See my detailed notes below in Section 3.\n\nDefine **\\\"Property\\\"** broadly to include all three tax parcels (460-12-04-007, 460-12-04-008, and 460-12-04-009) and the vacated Anson Lane strip. Define **\\\"Loan Documents\\\"** to cross-reference the GC contract with Beaufort Builders Group LLC (SC License No. SC-GC-47921), the renovation budget, and all reserve agreements. Define **\\\"Guarantor\\\"** as Neil Ashford Graydon, individually, of 14 Church Street, Charleston, SC 29401. Define **\\\"Maturity Date\\\"** as April 15, 2030.\n\n**[2. Granting Clause and Legal Description]{.underline}**\n\nUse the legal description from the title commitment issued by Prestige Title & Settlement Services LLC (Commitment No. PT-2025-04-1178, effective date February 28, 2025, underwritten by Commonwealth National Title Underwriters Inc.): Lots 7, 8, and 9 of the Hampstead Village Plat, recorded in Plat Book 41 at Page 218, in the RMC Office for Charleston County, South Carolina, together with the former Anson Lane strip vacated by City of Charleston Ordinance No. 2019-084, conveyed by quitclaim deed recorded in Deed Book 1128 at Page 443 in the aforesaid RMC Office.\n\n**CRITICAL --- ANSON LANE CHAIN OF TITLE.** Jordan --- the Anson Lane strip was conveyed to the borrower\\'s predecessor by quitclaim deed only (Deed Book 1128, Page 443). A quitclaim deed provides no warranties of title whatsoever. We need to verify three things before we can be comfortable with this parcel as mortgaged collateral: (1) that the title commitment from Prestige Title and Commonwealth National Title Underwriters Inc. affirmatively insures fee simple title to the Anson Lane strip without any Schedule B-II exception that carves it out or qualifies the estate as less than fee simple; (2) that the vesting chain from the City of Charleston through the quitclaim deed to the current seller and then to borrower is complete and unbroken, with no gaps in ownership or intervening claims; and (3) whether we should request a specific title endorsement --- an ALTA 9 endorsement or equivalent --- covering this parcel separately to ensure the lender has specific affirmative insurance against loss arising from a title defect in the strip. Review the title commitment Schedule B-II carefully. If the Anson Lane strip is excepted, shown as less than fee simple, or qualified in any way, we have a gap in collateral coverage. This is a non-trivial issue because the strip connects Lots 7 through 9 and its loss could impair vehicular and pedestrian access to the Property and undermine the approved site plan. Flag this for Prestige Title immediately and copy me on any correspondence.\n\nThe granting clause should convey a mortgage lien on fee simple title to the Property, together with all improvements now or hereafter erected thereon, all fixtures, building systems, equipment, and appurtenances. Include the standard \\\"together with\\\" language for rents, profits, issues, and all other collateral described in the mortgage. Reference all three tax map numbers in the granting clause itself, in addition to the full metes and bounds description in Exhibit A.\n\nNote: The legal description should be set out in **Exhibit A** to the mortgage and referenced in the granting clause by incorporation. We will need the surveyor\\'s legal description (metes and bounds) in addition to the plat reference --- confirm with the borrower\\'s surveyor that the ALTA/NSPS survey has been ordered and will be delivered in certified form before the closing date.\n\n**[3. Assignment of Leases, Rents, and Profits]{.underline}**\n\n**IMPORTANT --- ABSOLUTE ASSIGNMENT REQUIRED.** Our template currently uses a collateral assignment of rents, which is the older approach and is insufficient for this deal. South Carolina enacted the Uniform Assignment of Rents Act (S.C. Code § 29-3-100 *et seq.*), and we need to draft this provision as an **absolute assignment** of all leases, rents, issues, and profits of the Property, not merely a collateral assignment. Under the SC statute, an absolute assignment is effective upon execution and recording; the lender\\'s right to collect rents is perfected upon recording in the county land records but enforcement --- meaning actual collection of rents by the lender --- is triggered only by borrower default. Revise the template language throughout to remove any \\\"collateral\\\" or \\\"as additional security\\\" characterization and replace it with \\\"absolute and present\\\" assignment language. The operative provision should state affirmatively that borrower unconditionally and absolutely transfers and assigns to Lender all right, title, and interest in and to all current and future leases, subleases, and occupancy agreements affecting the Property, and all rents, income, revenues, issues, profits, and other sums now or hereafter payable thereunder, without reservation.\n\n**CRITICAL --- LOWCOUNTRY PROVISIONS LEASE --- NO ASSIGNMENT/CONSENT CLAUSE.** I\\'ve reviewed the lease abstracts from the rent roll in detail. The Lowcountry Provisions Inc. lease (Bay 1, approximately 3,800 SF, \\$28.50/SF NNN, lease expiration December 31, 2027) has no assignment or subletting provision whatsoever --- the lease abstract notes \\\"Assignment/Subletting: No provision.\\\" This is unusual for a commercial lease and creates a significant wrinkle. Under S.C. Code § 29-3-100 *et seq.*, the assignment of rents is enforceable against a tenant upon notification regardless of what the underlying lease says about assignment, so we are legally covered from a pure enforceability standpoint. However, a tenant whose lease contains no express consent-to-assignment clause could raise practical objections or withhold cooperation at the very moment we need to enforce --- and the last thing the bank wants in a workout is a tenant claiming it didn\\'t consent to paying rent to the lender.\n\nBest practice here requires three steps: **(a)** require borrower to represent and warrant in the mortgage that it has the full right and authority to assign all rents under all leases affecting the Property, including specifically and without limitation the Lowcountry Provisions Inc. lease; **(b)** require delivery of a **tenant estoppel certificate** from Lowcountry Provisions Inc. as a condition precedent to closing, in which the tenant acknowledges the existence of the mortgage, the assignment of rents to the lender, and agrees to pay rent to the lender upon written notice following a borrower default; and **(c)** include a post-closing covenant requiring borrower to use commercially reasonable efforts to obtain a lease amendment to the Lowcountry Provisions lease adding a standard assignment-consent provision at the next renewal, extension, or amendment opportunity. Flag this item for Marcus at the bank --- he should know that the Lowcountry Provisions estoppel is critical and should not be waived.\n\nThe other two occupied leases are lower-risk on this point but still require estoppels at closing:\n\n> • **Harmon & Wren Design Studio LLC** (Bay 3, approximately 3,800 SF, \\$24.00/SF modified gross, lease expiration June 30, 2026) --- the lease abstract shows a standard landlord assignment consent clause. Estoppel required at closing but lower risk.\n>\n> • **Tidemark Consulting Group LLC** (2nd Floor Suite A, approximately 9,800 SF, \\$22.00/SF NNN, lease expiration March 31, 2029) --- same, standard consent-to-assignment provision in the lease. Estoppel required at closing.\n\nThe mortgage should grant borrower a revocable license to collect rents until the occurrence and continuance of an Event of Default. Upon default, lender may collect rents directly upon written notice to tenants without the necessity of obtaining a court order or appointing a receiver, though we should also include the standard provisions for lender\\'s right to seek appointment of a receiver in any judicial proceeding to enforce the mortgage.\n\n**[4. Single-Purpose Entity Covenants]{.underline}**\n\n**CRITICAL --- SPE COVENANTS VS. OPERATING AGREEMENT.** The term sheet requires full single-purpose entity covenants for Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC. I\\'ve reviewed the redacted summary of the borrower\\'s operating agreement provided by borrower\\'s counsel, and it does **not** currently contain any of the following provisions, all of which we will need:\n\n**(1) Independent Director/Manager Requirement.** The operating agreement designates Graydon Development Group LLC as the sole managing member with exclusive management authority. There is no provision whatsoever for an independent director or independent manager whose affirmative consent is required before the borrower may file a voluntary bankruptcy petition, consent to an involuntary bankruptcy petition, dissolve, merge, consolidate, or wind up its affairs. We need this provision for bankruptcy remoteness --- without it, Graydon Development Group LLC could unilaterally file the borrower into bankruptcy, which defeats the entire purpose of the SPE structure.\n\n**(2) Separateness Covenants.** The operating agreement does not include standard separateness covenants. We need the borrower to covenant that it shall at all times: maintain separate books and records; maintain separate bank accounts; not commingle its assets with those of any affiliate; hold itself out to the public as a separate legal entity; use its own stationery, invoices, and checks; not guarantee or become obligated for the debts of any affiliate; pay its own liabilities from its own assets; maintain arm\\'s-length relationships with affiliates; and maintain adequate capitalization in light of its contemplated business.\n\n**(3) Restriction to Single Purpose.** The operating agreement\\'s purpose clause appears to allow \\\"any lawful business\\\" --- a standard boilerplate formulation. We need this narrowed to a single-purpose restriction: the borrower\\'s sole purpose is owning, operating, managing, leasing, and financing the Property at 520--534 King Street, Charleston, SC 29403, and activities incidental thereto.\n\n**(4) Member Consent.** Here is the complication. The borrower has three members with potentially divergent economic and governance interests: **Graydon Development Group LLC** (72% membership interest), **Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP** (18% membership interest), and **Harborline Capital Advisors LLC** (10% membership interest). The SPE covenants --- especially the independent director requirement and the restriction on voluntary bankruptcy filings --- must be binding on all members, not just the managing member. We need written confirmation that Tidewater and Harborline have reviewed the SPE restrictions, understand their implications, and have consented to be bound by them. The operating agreement summary I reviewed does not reflect any such consent.\n\n**ACTION ITEMS:**\n\n> \\(a\\) Draft the SPE covenants in the mortgage as though the operating agreement already contains compliant provisions --- that is, include representations and warranties that the borrower\\'s organizational documents contain SPE provisions satisfying the lender\\'s requirements.\n>\n> \\(b\\) Separately, prepare a form of **Operating Agreement Amendment** adding the independent director requirement, separateness covenants, single-purpose restriction, and unanimity requirement for bankruptcy filings, dissolution, and merger.\n>\n> \\(c\\) Make delivery of the executed Operating Agreement Amendment --- signed by **all three members** (Graydon Development Group LLC, Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP, and Harborline Capital Advisors LLC) --- a **condition precedent to closing**.\n>\n> \\(d\\) Include a covenant in the mortgage that borrower shall not amend, modify, or waive any SPE provision of its operating agreement without the lender\\'s prior written consent in each instance.\n\nOne additional note: **Tidewater GP Holdings LLC** is the general partner of Tidewater Opportunity Fund II LP. Confirm that Tidewater GP Holdings LLC has the authority to bind the fund to the operating agreement amendment. Check the limited partnership agreement or, if that is not available, request a GP authorization certificate or resolution prior to closing.\n\n**[5. Prepayment / Yield Maintenance]{.underline}**\n\nThe prepayment structure from the term sheet is as follows: a 24-month absolute lockout period (months 1--24), followed by yield maintenance (months 25--48), followed by open prepayment with no premium (months 49--60, i.e., the final 12 months before the April 15, 2030 Maturity Date).\n\n**CRITICAL --- YIELD MAINTENANCE FORMULA MUST BE PRECISE.** The term sheet describes the prepayment premium as \\\"yield maintenance (treasury rate + 50 bps spread)\\\" but does not specify several critical variables, each of which must be defined with precision in the mortgage:\n\n> **(a)** Which U.S. Treasury rate? The Constant Maturity Treasury (CMT) rate? If so, for what tenor --- matched to the remaining term of the loan from the prepayment date to maturity? Matched to the remaining yield maintenance period? Interpolated between two published tenors if no exact match exists?\n>\n> **(b)** How is the present value of remaining payments calculated? Is the discount rate the Treasury rate alone, or the Treasury rate plus the 50 basis point spread referenced in the term sheet?\n>\n> **(c)** Is the prepayment premium the greater of the yield maintenance amount or some fixed minimum percentage of the prepaid principal (e.g., 1%), or is it the yield maintenance amount standing alone with no minimum?\n>\n> **(d)** What happens if the calculated yield maintenance premium is zero or negative --- that is, if Treasury rates have risen sufficiently since origination that there is no present-value differential? Is there a floor of zero, or is there a minimum premium regardless?\n\n**South Carolina enforceability concern.** SC courts have generally enforced yield maintenance premiums in commercial loan transactions as reasonable liquidated damages provisions, but the formula must be specific, mathematically deterministic, and objectively calculable at the time of prepayment. If the formula is ambiguous or requires subjective judgment calls, a court could deem it an unenforceable penalty or refuse to enforce it on grounds of indefiniteness. We dealt with precisely this issue in a prior transaction approximately two years ago, and I do not want a repeat.\n\n**DRAFTING INSTRUCTION.** Insert a precise, self-contained yield maintenance formula in the mortgage substantially as follows:\n\n> *\\\"The Yield Maintenance Premium shall equal the positive excess, if any, of (A) the present value of all remaining scheduled monthly payments of interest and principal from the prepayment date through the last day of the Yield Maintenance Period (the payment due on or about month 48 following the date of the Note), discounted monthly at a per annum rate equal to the Treasury Rate, over (B) the outstanding principal amount being prepaid. The \\\"Treasury Rate\\\" shall mean the yield to maturity, as of the second Business Day preceding the prepayment date, of the United States Treasury security (as reported in Federal Reserve Statistical Release H.15 --- Selected Interest Rates, or any successor publication) having a maturity date closest to, but not exceeding, the last day of the Yield Maintenance Period. If no published maturity exactly matches, interpolate linearly between the two nearest published maturities. In no event shall the Yield Maintenance Premium be less than zero.\\\"*\n\nA key interpretive question: the 50 basis points referenced in the term sheet. Add the 50 bps to the **loan rate** (not to the Treasury rate) when computing the stream of remaining payments to be discounted --- that is, the remaining payments should be calculated at the Note rate, and the spread over Treasuries reflects the lender\\'s bargained-for return. Confirm this interpretation with Marcus before finalizing. The term sheet\\'s reference to \\\"treasury rate + 50 bps spread\\\" is ambiguous on its face and could reasonably be read either way.\n\nInclude the lockout provision as an **absolute prohibition** on voluntary prepayment during months 1 through 24, with no exception other than the application of casualty insurance proceeds or condemnation awards, which should be applied at par without any prepayment premium. Confirm this casualty/condemnation carve-out with the client --- some lenders want a premium even on involuntary prepayments, though that is the minority position.\n\n**[6. Additional Notes --- Other Sections Requiring Attention]{.underline}**\n\nThe following sections of the template require customization but are more straightforward. Brief notes on each:\n\n**Security Interest / UCC Collateral. Standard Article 9 language. Ensure we cover all personal property, fixtures (file as a fixture filing in the Charleston County RMC and as a UCC-1 financing statement with the SC Secretary of State), equipment, accounts, general intangibles, insurance proceeds, condemnation proceeds, permits, licenses, contracts (including the GC contract with Beaufort Builders Group LLC), and all reserve accounts held by the lender. The UCC-1 should name Palmetto Gateway Partners LLC (EIN: 84-6213750) as debtor and Calverley Heritage Bank as secured party. Confirm the collateral description in the UCC-1 matches the mortgage.**\n\n**Renovation Disbursement Provisions.** The holdback is \\$2,250,000 against a total renovation budget of \\$3,150,000 --- the term sheet contemplates periodic draws from the holdback upon satisfaction of disbursement conditions. Build in standard draw procedures: written draw request with not less than ten business days\\' advance notice, architect\\'s certification of percentage completion, unconditional lien waivers from Beaufort Builders Group LLC and all subcontractors for all work covered by prior draws, a title endorsement (no intervening liens since last draw), and 10% retainage until substantial completion. Substantial completion deadline is June 15, 2026. Cross-reference the completion guaranty from Neil Ashford Graydon, which guarantees the borrower\\'s obligation to complete the renovation on budget and on schedule.\n\n**Insurance Covenants.** Standard commercial property coverage: all-risk (special form) at full replacement cost, commercial general liability (\\$2,000,000 per occurrence / \\$5,000,000 aggregate), builder\\'s risk during the renovation period, and the reserve/escrow provisions for insurance premiums. The template should be fine here --- just update the coverage amounts and add the builder\\'s risk requirement for the construction phase.\n\n**Financial Covenants.** DSCR minimum of 1.25x, tested quarterly on a trailing twelve-month basis, beginning 24 months post-closing (first test date April 15, 2027). Guarantor minimum net worth of not less than \\$1,000,000, tested annually. Guarantor minimum liquid assets of not less than \\$500,000, tested annually. Audited financial statements of borrower within 120 days of fiscal year end. Quarterly rent rolls within 30 days of each calendar quarter end. These are all specified in the term sheet --- populate the template accordingly.\n\n**Default and Remedies.** Standard default provisions. Ensure we include SC-specific foreclosure language --- South Carolina is a judicial foreclosure state with no power of sale. The mortgage should not purport to grant a power of sale. Cross-default to the Note, the Guaranty Agreement, and the Environmental Indemnity Agreement. Default rate of interest: the applicable rate plus 5.00% per annum. Late charge: 5% of any installment of principal or interest not received within 10 calendar days of its due date.\n\n**Governing Law / Recording.** South Carolina law governs. The mortgage will be recorded in the Charleston County Register of Mesne Conveyance. Confirm documentary stamp tax and recording fee calculations with Prestige Title before closing. The mortgage tax in South Carolina is 0.37% of the indebtedness secured --- on \\$14,750,000, that is \\$54,575.00. Budget accordingly.\n\n**Environmental Covenants.** Use our standard template environmental compliance covenant. The Phase I ESA from Clearfield Environmental Consulting came back clean except for the controlled recognized environmental condition, which has a no-further-action letter on file. The Environmental Indemnity Agreement is a separate document executed by borrower and guarantor and will handle the substantive environmental risk allocation. Standard provisions in the mortgage should suffice.\n\n**[Closing Notes]{.underline}**\n\nJordan --- let me know if you have questions on any of the foregoing. Prioritize the assignment of rents restructuring (switching from collateral to absolute assignment), the SPE/operating agreement issue (including the form of operating agreement amendment), and the yield maintenance formula. Those are the three most complex drafting tasks and I want to see them handled correctly in the first draft. I want to see a clean initial draft by **March 28** so we have time for internal review before sending to Marcus on April 1.\n\nAlso, do not forget to confirm the Anson Lane chain of title with Prestige Title early in the process --- if there is a gap in the title to that strip, we need to know well before we get deep into document drafting and negotiate ourselves into a corner.\n\nOne more thought --- coordinate with Marcus on whether the bank\\'s loan committee or board has approved any exceptions to standard underwriting guidelines for this loan. The as-is LTV runs a little hot. He\\'ll know what I mean.\n\nKit\n\nCatherine \\\"Kit\\\" Ashworth Partner Fielding, Ashworth & Cole LLP Charleston & Savannah\n\ncc: File\n", "tool_call_id": "call_01_CIZ6sL11PtVpSJX7Bqff5597", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/prior-counsel-markup-notes.docx\"}"}
